# BusyBox inittab for the Magic Mirror.
#
# The whole point of running an init at all is the `respawn` line below: a
# crashed app restarts in well under a second. In v1 there was nothing beneath
# the application, so the only recovery mechanism available was a reboot.

# --- Filesystems ---
#
# These mirror Buildroot's default inittab, and they are not optional.
#
# An earlier version of this file omitted them, on the assumption that
# devtmpfs (which the kernel mounts itself) was all that mattered because
# /dev/fb0 worked. It was not: without /sys there is no /sys/class/net, so
# every check for the wireless interface failed and wlan0 was reported
# missing while the radio was in fact up and running firmware. Without /proc,
# modprobe, wpa_supplicant and hostapd are all working blind too.
#
# The symptom appeared four layers up as "could not start own network", which
# is a long way from "sysfs is not mounted".
::sysinit:/bin/mount -t proc proc /proc
::sysinit:/bin/mount -t sysfs sysfs /sys
::sysinit:/bin/mkdir -p /dev/pts /dev/shm
::sysinit:/bin/mount -a
::sysinit:/bin/mkdir -p /run/lock/subsys
::sysinit:/bin/hostname -F /etc/hostname

::sysinit:/etc/init.d/rcS

# The application, supervised. mm-supervise handles crash counting and
# rollback to the previous binary; see M9 in DESIGN.md.
::respawn:/sbin/mm-supervise

# A console on the UART, for when something has gone wrong enough that the
# screen is not telling you. Costs nothing when nothing is attached.
console::respawn:/sbin/getty -L console 0 vt100

::ctrlaltdel:/sbin/reboot
::shutdown:/etc/init.d/rcK
::shutdown:/bin/umount -a -r
